+# The Daily Grind
+
+A responsive coffee homepage with rewards registration through the browser Email Verification API. No OTP or magic-link email is sent. Membership is saved only after email ownership is cryptographically verified on the server.
+
+## Configuration
+
+Requires Node.js 22.9+ and pnpm. Install dependencies with `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile`. Copy `.env.example` to `.env` and set:
+
+- `APP_ORIGIN`: exact public site origin, including scheme, without a trailing slash. Use HTTPS for deployment. Never derive this security boundary from request headers.
+- `EMAIL_VERIFICATION_ORIGIN_TRIAL_TOKEN`: register the actual deployment origin for Chrome's Email Verification origin trial and supply its current token. The server emits it as an `Origin-Trial` response header on the homepage.
+- `DATA_DIR`: a writable, persistent directory for member records, outside any publicly served files.
+- `PORT`: defaults to 11222.
+
+`pnpm start` is the runtime command. It loads `.env` when present. Set `NODE_ENV=production` in deployment to enforce an HTTPS origin. Terminate HTTPS at a trusted reverse proxy forwarding to this Node process.
+
+The browser feature currently requires a participating provider, a supported Chrome origin-trial build, and an active provider session in the same browser profile. Without a valid token, registration explains the requirements and offers a fresh attempt. It never claims verification or creates an unverified member. There is deliberately no email-based fallback.
+
+See [Chrome's Email Verification documentation](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/identity/email-verification) and the [server verification guide](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/modern-web-guidance/guides/security/verify-email-ownership). Origin-trial availability, token renewals, and experimental protocol details can change.
+
+## Server flow
+
+1. `GET /` renders a random per-form nonce and form identifier. The expected nonce stays in memory, keyed by form identifier and bound to an HttpOnly, SameSite session cookie (Secure over HTTPS). Pages have `Cache-Control: no-store`.
+2. The email and hidden `autocomplete="email-verification-token"` inputs are in the same form. Chrome supplies the key-bound token during submission.
+3. `POST /api/verify-email` consumes the challenge before asynchronous work. It checks the request origin, session, expiry, names, and email. Missing or invalid tokens return an actionable failure; the client reloads for a new challenge.
+4. The endpoint checks DNS issuer delegation, HTTPS issuer discovery, issuer signatures, email and verification claims, expiry/freshness, and browser key binding to the nonce and configured site origin. SD-JWT parsing and cryptographic verification use `@sd-jwt/core` and `jose`. Provider fetches reject private/reserved network destinations and redirects.
+5. Verified member details and marketing consent are written to `DATA_DIR/members.json`. Duplicate email registration does not overwrite an existing membership. Responses do not reveal whether the address already existed.
+
+The included JSON store and in-memory challenges/rate limits support **one server process**. Restarts expire pending forms. For multiple instances or higher traffic, replace these with a shared transactional database, atomic challenge consumption, and shared rate limiting. Member creation is implemented; returning-member authentication, purchase tracking, and points redemption are outside this signup flow.
+
+## Validation
+
+- `pnpm test`: pure handler and cryptographic fixture tests, with no listening server or server-entrypoint import.
+- `pnpm build`: JavaScript syntax checks; this vanilla site requires no bundling.
+
+The tests cover positive verification, invalid signatures, email mismatch, unverified claims, expiry, nonce/audience/hash binding, DNS delegation, multiple issuer keys without `kid`, unsafe fetch destinations, replay, secure cookie rendering, and persistent duplicate prevention.
